---
// /for-orgs/claim/ - taking over an organization somebody else submitted
// (features/ORG_ONBOARDING.md, #1539).
//
// THIS IS THE MIGRATION PATH FOR THE REGISTRY WE ALREADY HAVE, not a new
// concept. `pipeline/sources.json` registers 33 sources across nine
// organizations (counted 2026-08-27, SOURCE_REGISTRY.md), every one of them
// put there by a maintainer rather than by the organization - which is
// exactly what `Org.state == unclaimed` means: live trails, no admins.
//
// TWO THINGS THE PAGE HAS TO SAY OUT LOUD, because a trails chair arriving
// here is worried about both:
//
// 1. **Claiming does not change what is published.** Their sections have been
//    on the map for months and hikers have walked them. Claiming puts them in
//    charge of what happens next; it does not alter a line.
// 2. **First is not owner.** They become the first of three. Nothing
//    publishes until two more admins from their org join them - the same rule
//    as an org that signed itself up, because it is the same rule.
//
// AND THE ONE THE PAGE OWES SOMEBODY WHO IS NOT ACTING IN GOOD FAITH'S
// VICTIM: a disputed claim is frozen for a person, with no automatic exit. A
// timer would resolve a contested claim in favour of whoever was patient.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import OrgNav from '../../components/OrgNav.astro'

const title = 'OurHike — Claim your org'
const description =
  "Somebody put your trails on OurHike before you got here. If you have an email at your org's domain, that is enough to take it over."
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <OrgNav />
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <span class="org-eyebrow">ALREADY ON OURHIKE · NO ADMINS YET</span>
      <h1 class="org-form__title">Claim your org</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Somebody put your trails on OurHike before you got here. They are live
        and anybody can walk them — but nobody from your org controls them yet.
        If you have an email at your org's domain, that is enough to take it
        over.
      </p>

      <form class="org-form" id="claim-form" novalidate style="margin-top: 24px">
        <fieldset class="org-form__group">
          <span class="org-eyebrow">STEP 1 · FIND IT</span>
          <label class="org-label" for="claim-search">Search by name or website</label>
          <input
            class="org-input"
            id="claim-search"
            name="search"
            type="search"
            placeholder="carolinamountainclub.org"
            autocomplete="off"
          />
          <ul class="org-results" id="claim-results" role="status" aria-live="polite"></ul>
          <p class="org-help">
            Cannot find yours? Nobody has submitted it —
            <a href="/for-orgs/">register it yourself</a> instead.
          </p>
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset class="org-form__group">
          <span class="org-eyebrow">STEP 2 · PROVE IT IS YOURS</span>
          <span class="org-label">An email at your domain is the key</span>
          <p class="org-help">
            We already check this when an org signs itself up — at least one
            admin has to be on the org's own domain. Same rule here, doing the
            same job. It is the address your account is signed in with, not one
            typed into this form: an address somebody types proves nothing.
          </p>
          <p class="org-identity" id="claim-identity" data-state="signed-out">
            <span class="org-identity__out">
              <strong>Sign in with your org address.</strong>
              <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--small" href="/app/?intent=claim-org"
                >Sign in to continue</a
              >
            </span>
            <span class="org-identity__in" hidden>
              Signed in as <strong id="claim-identity-email"></strong> ·
              <a href="/app/?intent=switch-account">not your org? switch account</a>
            </span>
          </p>
        </fieldset>

        <button class="btn org-form__submit" id="claim-submit" type="submit" disabled>
          This one is mine
        </button>
      </form>

      <div class="org-steps" style="margin-top: 32px">
        <span class="org-eyebrow">WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU DO</span>
        <ol class="org-numbered">
          <li>
            <strong>Your address is the check.</strong> A working address at your
            org's domain is what makes you one of them. There is no form to fill
            in and nobody to email about it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You become the first admin — not the owner.</strong> The
            first of three. You can edit and propose, but nothing publishes
            until two more admins from your org join you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Name your other two.</strong> Invite two colleagues and the
            org is fully yours. Until then the submitted data stays live and
            unchanged.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="org-note" style="margin-top: 26px">
        <p>
          <strong>Your trails are already live.</strong> If somebody submitted
          your sections months ago, hikers have been finding them there ever
          since. Claiming the org does not change what is published — it puts
          you in charge of what happens to it next.
        </p>
        <p class="org-note__warning">
          <strong>If somebody claims an org they should not have.</strong> A
          claim needs a working address at the org's own domain, and a second
          claim on the same org <strong>freezes it for a person to look at</strong>
          rather than resolving itself. There is deliberately no timer: a
          contested claim settled by a countdown is a claim settled in favour of
          whoever was patient.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script src="../../scripts/claimOrgForm.js"></script>
</Base>
